{
  "an5.192:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 5.192 ",
  "an5.192:0.2": "20. Brahmins ",
  "an5.192:0.3": "With the Brahmin Doṇa ",
  "an5.192:1.1": "Then Doṇa the brahmin went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. ",
  "an5.192:1.2": "When the greetings and polite conversation were over, Doṇa sat down to one side, and said to the Buddha: ",
  "an5.192:2.1": "“Worthy Gotama, I have heard that ",
  "an5.192:2.2": "the ascetic Gotama doesn’t bow to old brahmins, the elderly and senior, who are advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life; nor does he rise in their presence or offer them a seat. ",
  "an5.192:2.3": "And this is indeed the case, ",
  "an5.192:2.4": "for the worthy Gotama does not bow to old brahmins, elderly and senior, who are advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life; nor does he rise in their presence or offer them a seat. ",
  "an5.192:2.5": "This is not appropriate, worthy Gotama.” ",
  "an5.192:2.6": "“Doṇa, do you too claim to be a brahmin?” ",
  "an5.192:2.7": "“Worthy Gotama, if anyone should be rightly called ",
  "an5.192:2.8": "a brahmin, it’s me. ",
  "an5.192:2.9": "",
  "an5.192:2.10": "",
  "an5.192:2.11": "For I am well born on both my mother’s and father’s side, stemming from a clean vessel, with irrefutable and impeccable genealogy back to the seventh paternal generation. I recite and remember the hymns, and have mastered the three Vedas, together with their vocabularies and ritual performance, their phonology and w